cartridge "KICKER/J.KRAUSE+M.TUTTLE/1986"

' KICKER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Kicker" by John Krause and Mark Tuttle,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette February 1986 (Commodore 64 machine language, 1,576
' bytes started with SYS 2061; a two-player table-soccer game). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/kicker.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is built
' for the ColecoVision: a table-soccer pitch seen from above with steel
' rods and round men in red and blue, the kicking men as smooth sprites,
' a lit net when a goal goes in, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: each side has three rods of three men. Hold the stick up,
' centre or down to take your top, middle or bottom rod; left and right
' slide that rod. Press the button and the rod's men swing: a ball they
' touch in the first moments of the swing is kicked toward the other goal.
' The ball passes through the men of rods that are not swinging. First to
' 9 goals wins. Blue (controller 1) kicks down and scores in the bottom
' goal; red kicks up and scores at the top.
'
' From the original (orig/kicker.dis), in the C64's own pixels:
' - the ball moves in 1/256 pixel steps on each axis (speed bytes $02 and
'   $9B, fraction bytes $FB and $03) once per pass of the main loop; the
'   loop's delay (DEX x 256) makes about 12 passes per 60 Hz frame (LPF);
' - ball Y 64..222 and X 110..255; at a side or end wall it turns back,
'   its speed on that axis halved (OR 1) and a click sounds; reaching Y 222
'   with X 173..193 is a goal for blue, Y 64 the same for red;
' - friction: a speed of 2 or more loses 1 every 256 passes;
' - every 20 passes the sticks are read: up / centre / down take rod 0, 2,
'   4 (blue) or 1, 3, 5 (red), left / right slide it one step (8 pixels)
'   within 0..6; the men stand 40 pixels apart from X 117 + 8 x step; rods
'   at Y 76, 100, 124, 148, 172, 196;
' - the button shows the rod's men (sprites) while held; a kick counts only
'   while the swing timer (4, counted down every 20 passes from the press)
'   is not 0; blue wins a touch of both sides;
' - a kick: speed toward the goal = the speed mask (SLOW $1F, MEDIUM $3F,
'   FAST $7F), sideways speed = random AND mask, random side;
' - after a goal the ball rests at Y 143, X 183 with no speed; 9 goals end.
'
' Liberties: collisions are tested once per frame (the C64's sprite
' collision register also latches once per frame) with the drawn shapes'
' rows (art.py: the men are 14 x 16, the C64's 14 x 18 multicolour men;
' blue's are drawn 4 lines down so their shoe ends where the C64's did,
' which lets blue reach the kick-off spot as on the C64);
' the goal flash lasts 160 frames (C64 about 2.6 s, 16 delays of 0.16 s)
' and lights the net at 3 Hz; markers beside the pitch show which rod each
' side holds (the C64 showed nothing until the button); one player against
' the computer (it plays blue) at three skills chosen on the title: EASY
' looks at the ball every 14 frames, takes a neighbouring rod every fourth
' look, skips its swings for one look in four (the frame clock decides)
' and has the narrowest swing window; NORMAL every 14 frames; HARD every
' 12 frames with the widest swing window; besides the
' original two-player game; players and speed chosen on the title with the
' stick; GAME OVER then the title (the C64 waited for a key and asked the
' speed again).
'
' Controls: two players: controller 1 blue, controller 2 red. One player:
' controller 1 plays red. Stick up / centre / down: rod; left / right:
' slide; either button: kick. On the title, up and down choose a line,
' left and right change it, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

